      "text": "To determine how many square inches is the area of a circle greater than that of a square, we can use the triangle inequality theorem to find the height of the square.\n\nThe diagonal of the square is the hypotenuse of the right triangle, which has base $x$ and height $y$. Since the diagonal of the square is 5 inches, we know that $x^2 + y^2 = 25$.\n\nBy the Pythagorean theorem, we have $x^2 + y^2 = 25$.\n\nWe also know that $x = y$, so we can rewrite $x^2 + y^2 = 5$. Since $x = y$, we have $2x^2 = 5$. Therefore, $x = \\sqrt{2.5} = 1.5$ inches.\n\nNow, the height of the square is $2(y) = 2(1.5) = 3$ inches.\n\nThe area of the circle is $\\pi \\cdot r^2$, where $r$ is the radius of the circle. The diameter of the circle is the hypotenuse of a right triangle, which has one angle of 45 degrees. Since this forms a 45-45-90 triangle, the other two angles are each 45 degrees, so each side opposite a 45-degree angle is equal to the hypotenuse of the other triangle.\n\nSince the hypotenuse of the right triangle is 5 inches, the length of each leg of the triangle is $\\frac{5}{2} = 2.5$ inches.\n\nTherefore, the radius of the circle is 2.5 inches, and the area of the circle is $\\pi \\cdot 2.5^2 = 6.25 \\pi$ square inches.\n\nThe area of the square is $x^2 = 3^2 = 9$ square inches.\n\nTherefore, the area of the circle is greater than the area of the square by $6.25 \\pi - 9 \\approx 6.25(3.14159) - 9 = 19.62657 - 9 = \\boxed{10.6}$ square inches.\n\nThe answer is: 10.6",
